          ":question/id": "5342a760-4f6b-57f7-989c-35eadd9600b3",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-143533",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and  $\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\right\\}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Since $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{3} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=3=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$  \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,a,b\\in R\\right\\}=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement \u201c$A$ is invertible\u201d is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible we must have   $det(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $det(A)=0$ only."
